// ============================================================================
// WHAT THIS CODE DOES (big picture)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This is a single "custom code" step inside a HubSpot workflow. It runs
// whenever a contact reaches this step, and its job is to:
//
//   1. Look up all "Campaign Qualification" (CQ) records tied to that contact
//      that represent a live, unexpired special offer.
//   2. Throw out any CQs tied to buyers we've decided NOT to make offers for
//      (the EXCLUDED_BUYERS list below).
//   3. Figure out which AO Campaign (custom object) each remaining CQ belongs
//      to, whether that campaign is the "Lapse & Attrited text AO campaign"
//      (top priority, no buyer restriction) or the "ENT Retail AO" campaign
//      (second priority, approved retail buyers only), and how that
//      campaign's `campaign_config` says it's set up (in particular,
//      `draftOfferAssets[].offerTypes` — whether the campaign is configured
//      to generate more than one offer type, e.g. both PREFERRED and
//      EXPRESS_ACCEPT, per qualifying run).
//   4. Pick the winning CQ in this order: any CQ from the Lapse & Attrited
//      campaign always wins first. Otherwise, if there are CQs in the
//      priority Retail campaign AND the buyer is on our approved retail
//      list, pick the best one of those. Otherwise, pick the best CQ from
//      all the other ("non-retail") campaigns.
//   5. Record a `cq_match_status` describing how that pick was made — see
//      "MATCH STATUS RULES" below for exactly what each value means.
//   6. Look up a logo image for the winning buyer.
//   7. Build the side-by-side draft offers table HTML. If the winning CQ's
//      campaign is configured for more than one offer type, this pulls in
//      every other CQ from that SAME campaign and the SAME campaign run
//      (matched via `source_run_started_at`) for this contact, and renders
//      one column per offer type found. Otherwise the table is just the one
//      winning offer.
//   8. Hand back a big bundle of information (the "outputFields") that the
//      rest of the HubSpot workflow/email/page uses to personalize what the
//      contact sees (buyer name, discount amount, logo, offers table, etc).
//
// If at any point there's nothing usable to show (no CQs, all excluded,
// missing required info), it hands back a safe "empty" set of fields instead
// of guessing.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MATCH STATUS RULES (cq_match_status)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   "Lapse & Attrited text AO campaign" -> the winner came from this
//                                campaign. It outranks EVERYTHING else,
//                                including the Retail priority campaign, and
//                                unlike Retail there's no buyer allowlist —
//                                any (non-excluded) CQ from this campaign
//                                wins automatically.
//   "ENT Retail AO campaign"  -> the winner came from the priority Retail
//                                campaign pool (an approved retail buyer's CQ
//                                in that campaign). Wins outright over
//                                everything below, but loses to the Lapse &
//                                Attrited campaign above.
//   "multiple_matches"        -> the winning CQ's campaign is configured
//                                (via campaign_config.draftOfferAssets) to
//                                produce more than one offer type, AND more
//                                than one CQ from that exact campaign run
//                                actually turned up for this contact. This is
//                                the ONLY situation that produces this
//                                status — it reflects the campaign
//                                deliberately generating a Preferred +
//                                One-Time pair, not a real conflict. The
//                                offers table is built from that whole group.
//   "one_match"               -> every other case where a winner was found
//                                outside the priority campaigns: whether
//                                exactly one non-retail CQ qualified, or
//                                several did but they weren't a same-
//                                campaign/same-run multi-offer-type pair
//                                (different buyers/campaigns, a stray
//                                duplicate, CQs from two different runs of
//                                the same recurring campaign, etc). Either
//                                way, downstream treats this the same: send
//                                the single-offer email for whichever CQ won
//                                the priority + recency tie-break.
//   "none"                    -> no qualifying CQ at all (or the only
//                                candidates were in the priority campaign but
//                                for a non-approved buyer, which counts as no
//                                usable candidate).
// ============================================================================

